{
  "id": "py-27-changing-lists",
  "track": "python",
  "unit": "py-u5",
  "order": 27,
  "title": "Changing lists",
  "summary": "Unlike text, a list can be changed: replace an item, add with append and insert, take out with pop, remove and del.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 25,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "cells",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "A string can never be changed, but a list **can**. The same list object grows, shrinks and swaps items:\n\n- `xs[1] = 6` puts 6 at position 1, replacing what was there\n- `xs.append(8)` adds 8 at the **end**\n- `xs.insert(0, 2)` puts 2 at position 0; every item after it **slides right** and its position goes up by one\n- `xs.pop()` takes the **last** item out and gives it back; `xs.pop(0)` takes the first\n- `xs.remove(\"milk\")` takes out the first item **equal** to milk\n- `del xs[0]` deletes the item at a position\n\nWatch the **cells** panel: new boxes light up green, a replaced box shows what it **was**, and removed items are listed under the row as the others slide left.\n\nOne trap: these methods change the list and give back **None** (only `pop` gives back the item). So never write `xs = xs.append(8)`. That stores None in xs.",
    "analogy": "A queue of people. Someone can join at the end (append), cut in at a spot (insert), or step out (pop or remove), and everyone behind them shuffles along. It is still the same queue."
  },
